    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: `BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a human-readable `telephoneDisplay` field alongside the raw E.164 `telephone`, wired through the same OpenAPI \u2192 generated-DTO \u2192 MapStruct pipeline the other derived owner fields use.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `telephoneDisplay` string property to the `Owner` schema (so the code generator produces the DTO field/getter/setter).\n\n2. **`.../rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java`** \u2014 Made the class `public` and added a `public static String display(String e164)` method. It splits the stored E.164 number into country code (identified longest-prefix-first from the existing `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` table, falling back to the first two digits for unknown codes) and national digits, then re-joins as `\"+<code> <national grouped in threes>\"`. So `\"+61412345678\"` \u2192 `\"+61 412 345 678\"`. Non-E.164 input is returned unchanged.\n\n3. **`.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping` expression populating `telephoneDisplay` from `TelephoneE164.display(owner.getTelephone())`, matching how `locality`, `checkDigit`, etc. are derived.\n\nThe raw `telephone` still maps straight through as stored E.164, exactly as the test `coreFormatsTelephoneDisplay` asserts (`$.telephone` = `+61412345678`, `$.telephoneDisplay` = `+61 412 345 678`).",
